  "generated_text": "The direction vector of the first line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -k \\\\ k \\end{pmatrix}\\), and the direction vector of the second line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1/2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). For the lines to be coplanar, their direction vectors must be proportional. Thus, we have\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -k \\\\ k \\end{pmatrix} = t \\begin{pmatrix} 1/2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix},\\]for some scalar \\(t\\). This gives us the system of equations:\n\\[1 = \\frac{t}{2},\\]\n\\[-k = t,\\]\n\\[k = -t.\\]\n\nFrom the first equation, we have \\(t = 2\\). Substituting \\(t = 2\\) into the second equation, we get\n\\[-k = 2.\\]\nThus, \\(k = -2\\). Now, substituting \\(k = -2\\) into the third equation, we get\n\\[-2 = -2.\\]\nThis confirms that \\(k = -2\\).\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(k\\) is \\(\\boxed{-2}\\).",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[-2, '-2']",
    "gold_answer": "First, we check if the two lines can intersect.  For the two lines to intersect, we must have\n\\begin{align*}\n-1 + s &= \\frac{t}{2}, \\\\\n3 - ks &= 1 + t, \\\\\n1 + ks &= 2 - t.\n\\end{align*}Adding the second equation and third equation, we get $4 = 3,$ contradiction.  Thus, the two lines cannot intersect.\n\nSo for the two lines to be coplanar, the only other possibility is that they are parallel.  For the two lines to be parallel, their direction vectors must be proportional.  The direction vectors of the lines are $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -k \\\\ k \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 1/2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix},$ respectively.  These vectors are proportional when\n\\[2 = -k.\\]Hence, $k = \\boxed{-2}.$",
    "is_correct": true
  },
